<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<StyleSet xmlns="http://adamantium/ui"
          xmlns:x="http://adamantium/ui/xaml/extensions">

    <!-- Plain text. This file was EMPTY for most of its life, and the reason it no longer has to be is worth stating,
         because the old reason was a good one.

         A setter here used to land at Style priority, which outranks INHERITED - and inheritance is the channel a
         control uses to recolour its OWN content when its state changes (a selected row pushing the accent onto its
         presenter, the text below following). So a default colour on TextBlock silently stopped every selected row,
         pressed button and disabled label from following its state, everywhere in the application, with nothing in any
         log to say so. The theme's answer was to have no style at all and let the window seed the colour by
         inheritance - which worked, and left the framework one natural style away from breaking.

         A selector that narrows by NOTHING but the type is now filed as exactly what it is: a default FOR THAT TYPE
         (ValuePriority.TypeDefault), weaker than anything a nearer ancestor actually says. So this style dresses loose
         text and steps aside inside a control. Both halves are pinned in TypeDefaultSlotTests. -->

    <Style Selector="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{ObservableResource TextFillColorPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{ResourceReference FontSizeBody}"/>
    </Style>

</StyleSet>
